1. { -KEY- } Ke2 $1 ( { To take the opposition on the next move. } 1. Kd2 $2 Kd4 2. Ke2 e3 $1 3. fxe3 Ke4 { and Black enters decisively with the king's next turn, for example, } 4. Kd2 Kf3 5. Kd3 e5 { with zugzwang. In fact, we will later reach the same position in the main line, but with Black to move, which will make a huge difference! } ) 
Kd4 2. Kd2 e3+ ( 2... e5 3. Ke2 e3 4. fxe3 Ke4 5. Kd2 $1 { transposes to the main line. } ) 
3. fxe3+ Ke4 4. Ke2 e5 5. Kd2 $1 ( { White assures counterplay with the king along the d-file. } 5. Kf2 $2 Kd3 6. Kf3 e4 7. Kf2 Kd2 { and Black wins. } ) 
Kf3 6. Kd3 Kg3 ( 6... e4 $4 7. Kd4 { would even let White win. } ) 
7. Ke4 Kxh3 8. Kxe5 Kxg4 9. e4 h5 10. Kd6 ( { The best square for the King, in order to attack the b5-pawn later on. Instead } 10. Kd5 $2 h4 { would let Black queen with check, while } 10. Kd4 $2 { leads to the same thing after } h4 11. e5 Kf5 $1 { Smyslov's trick, which we will see more of in the chapter about Passed Pawns Races } 12. Kd5 h3 ) 
 ( { Finally, } 10. Kf6 $6 { admittedly lets White Queen, however the king would then need more time to approach the b5-pawn. } ) 
h4 11. e5 h3 12. e6 h2 13. e7 h1=Q 14. e8=Q { With excellent drawing chances. } 
